{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1420239660,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIxMzU4OTAyMQ=="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1420239660,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIxMzU4OTAyMg=="},"added":["annex"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1420567071,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY4OTAzNzk3","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/31#issuecomment-68903797"},"message":"Well, git-annex doesn't have S3 functionality inside. It uses a library.\nI think that using a library is a good solution.\n\nI mean, look at git-annex's code to retrieve a file from S3:\n\nretrieve :: S3Handle -\u003e Retriever\nretrieve h = fileRetriever $ \\f k p -\u003e liftIO $ runResourceT $ do\n        (fr, fh) \u003c- allocate (openFile f WriteMode) hClose\n        let req = S3.getObject (bucket info) (bucketObject info k)\n        S3.GetObjectResponse { S3.gorResponse = rsp } \u003c- sendS3Handle' h req\n        responseBody rsp $$+- sinkprogressfile fh p zeroBytesProcessed\n        release fr\n  where\n        info = hinfo h\n        sinkprogressfile fh meterupdate sofar = do\n                mbs \u003c- await\n                case mbs of\n                        Nothing -\u003e return ()\n                        Just bs -\u003e do\n                                let sofar' = addBytesProcessed sofar (S.length bs)\n                                liftIO $ do\n                                        void $ meterupdate sofar'\n                                        S.hPut fh bs\n                                sinkprogressfile fh meterupdate sofar'\n\nOnly 2 lines of that have anything to do with S3. The entire rest of it\nis to do with streaming the bytes out to a file, with progress meter update.\n\nAnd all of it is written to the abstraction level git-annex needs, which\nis \"retrieve this Key with progress sent to this MeterUpdate, and\nprovide a ContentSource to the passed callback action to consume it\",\nwhich is not the abstraction level a general-purpose library would need.\n\n(Nor is it a stable abstraction, all this has been massively reworked once in\nthe last year, and may well be again. I want flexability of implementation\ninternals so I can make them better; exposing a library API is counter to\nthat.)\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1420729464,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY5MTkwOTg5","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/31#issuecomment-69190989"},"message":"\"It uses a library. I think that using a library is a good solution\"\nyes -- it is a great solution to use a library whenever it is available ;)  \nWhat I meant is that to provide a custom downloader from S3 we will also need to use some library.  But by 'using' a library git-annex already has all the needed functionality built-in to provide e.g. fetches from S3.  Moreover you have here already a \"proper\" progress meter update for git-annex.   Wouldn't it be neat then (this is all just a food for thought, feel free just to say No and close) if from within my custom special remote I could rely on git-annex'es built-in functionality (thus with \"standard\" progress metter) to fetch from S3 instead of relying on 1 more library (e.g. s3cmd) on my end?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1434050300,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDExMTI0OTYxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-111249617"},"message":"Had a look at the details of removing the objectdir, and have put an implementation in a branch, removeobjectdir.\n\nAs noted above, freezing the hash directories can lead to races, and it would need a lock file. At worst, such a race would end with a permissions problem trying to write to a hash directory that had been frozen by another process. Not a big problem, and adding lock files wouldn't be too bad.\n\nBut there's also the problem that getKeysPresent walks .git/annex/objects to find  objects. However, object files are not the only files in there; there are also lock files, direct mode mapping files, etc. So, it currently looks for .git/annex/objects/xx/yy/$k/ and then checks if $k/$k exists. Without the $k directory, it can't look for $k/$k and so would need to find the files in .git/annex/objects/x/yy/ that are objects, and not locks etc. But, this cannot be determined from looking at the filename alone; \"SHAE1-xxxx.lock\" might be a lock file, or it might be an annexed file that happened to have the \".lock\" extension.\n\nSo, seems that to implement this, the repo layout would need to put all the object files\nin one set of hash directories, and the ancillary files elsewhere. Which becomes rather more complicated..","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1434050810,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDExMTI1MzEyMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-111253121"},"message":"Hmm, another reason it would need the lock files etc separated from the object files is, if a given filename can be either a lock file or an object file, than a repo could conceivably try to use the same filename for both purposes!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1434091031,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDExMTM3OTY2Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-111379666"},"message":"I might be missing smth but why not to have something like a single .git/annex/objects/lock   which would list the file (or files if somehow done in batch mode + may be a PID of the process) which being modified (i.e.their yy directories given write permissions)?  then upon any operation there could even be a check if such file exists?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1434131166,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDExMTU3MTU3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-111571572"},"message":"Urk, getKeysPresent has more problems without per-object directories. If the lock/inodecache/mapping files are moved out of the way, then only object files will be in .git/annex/objects/, and it would make sense for getKeysPresent to just walk those. But, in direct mode, the object files won't be located there.\n\nSo, it would need to instead walk, I suppose, the inode cache files, when in direct mode without per-object directories. Except, it's possible for direct mode repos to have some objects stored in .git/annex/objects (ones that are not currently in the work tree). And, such objects are not going to have inode cache files.\n\nSo, it seems it would need to walk both the inode cache files, and whatever objects are in .git/annex/objects. But then it might find a key in both locations (perhaps due to a race or a stale inode cache file etc), and return a key twice. And since getKeysPresent streams lazily, it can't dedup without becoming a lot more memory and cpu expensive.\n\nThis is rapidly looking to be a hell of a mess. And it's not particularly clear that the resulting pile of hacks and extra work, extra locking, etc is going to be faster than the relatively simple and clean current implementation. I have to wonder how much a filesystem that is less slow than ZFS would improve the numbers.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1434133706,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDExMTU4MTYxMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-111581611"},"message":"If http://git-annex.branchable.com/todo/cache_key_info/ were implemented, getKeysPresent could use its database of keys that are present in the annex.\n\nSo, this could be considered to be blocked by http://git-annex.branchable.com/design/caching_database/","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1434135233,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDExMTU4ODAyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-111588025"},"message":"regarding other FSs: there also will be about double boost on such operations such as du (see e.g. above https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-70953059 or here is a full dump of different reports: http://www.onerussian.com/tmp/benchs.txt )","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1444511274,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDE0NzEyNTgzNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-147125837"},"message":"just a note that if we thought that we would keep hitting filename length limits only under windows/FAT -- not quite so... backup to encfs'ed (fuse) directory started to fail:\n\n```\nls: cannot access /home/xxx/_backups_/vault/c/xxxyyyyy/.git/annex/objects/mq/8m/URL--http\u0026c%%audio.xxxyyyyy.net%audio%Prikliuchenia_Petrova_i_Vasechkina%plastinka2_-_K_nam_edet_huligan%\u0026s28pesnia_iz_kf\u0026s29_ne_verish.-33faff0bf383af1619db7ce9c5d9c04d/.URL--http\u0026c%%audio.xxxyyyyy.net%audio%Prikliuchenia_Petrova_i_Vasechkina%plastinka2_-_K_nam_edet_huligan%\u0026s28pesnia_iz_kf\u0026s29_ne_verish.-33faff0bf383af1619db7ce9c5d9c04d.2BfYyl: File name too long\n```\n\nwithout intermediate key directory I had higher chance to succeed ;)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1456434136,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDE4ODk4NTkwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/32#issuecomment-188985908"},"message":"For the reference -- https://git-annex.branchable.com/design/new_repo_versions/ describes new and future .git/annex store formats (to replace posted above link to v6)","files":null}]}